1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,190
Coming from C++ or PHP, you write a stack
class in Python. You do not need it.

2
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:09,030
Python already has one.

3
00:00:09,867 --> 00:00:16,283
A list is the stack. Append pushes. [-1]
peeks at the top. Pop takes the last item.

4
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:21,737
All of it happens at the end of the
list, so nothing else has to move.

5
00:00:22,567 --> 00:00:24,473
A queue takes from the front.

6
00:00:24,523 --> 00:00:27,914
With a list, that is pop(0).
The answer is correct.

7
00:00:27,964 --> 00:00:31,085
But every item behind it
shifts one place left.

8
00:00:31,135 --> 00:00:33,918
Drain two hundred thousand items this way,

9
00:00:33,968 --> 00:00:37,157
and on this machine it
takes about five seconds.

10
00:00:37,993 --> 00:00:41,033
So use a deque, from
the collections module.

11
00:00:41,083 --> 00:00:44,545
popleft() takes the front,
and nothing else moves.

12
00:00:44,595 --> 00:00:47,143
The same job takes nine milliseconds.

13
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:51,635
So when do you write the
class? When it adds something.

14
00:00:51,685 --> 00:00:53,871
To limit callers to push and pop.

15
00:00:53,921 --> 00:00:58,070
Or to add behaviour, like a stack
that also knows its minimum.

16
00:00:58,893 --> 00:01:03,249
So. For a stack, use a list.
For a queue, use a deque.

17
00:01:03,299 --> 00:01:07,083
And write a class only
when it earns its place.
